														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3712289">
									

<p>I can see where you are coming from. But this skip is ultimately about (ordinary human) relationships &#8212; how they are inevitably based on the construction of fictions we tell ourselves about our partner; how we need someone not just to love us, but also occasionally disapprove of us; and how, despite the artifice of it all, the loss of the fictions we tell ourselves can be tragic. Because this is the main point here, I didn't really want to explore the various permutations of what can happen with this anomaly.</p>
<p>I have answers in my head about attempting to contain the rabbit population [1], but I'm not sure if they should be edited in, for the same reason as above &#8212; this skip is meant to focus on something else. I guess it depends on how big of an omission this is; let's see if other readers have the same reaction.</p>
<p>[1] Rabbits can migrate, you miss one or two and they breed like, well, rabbits, so in a few years you've got the same problem again; dealing with this requires a large scale effort (you've got to comb every inch of half of Georgia), and the Foundation finds it easier to amnesticize ~20 people per year affected by this.</p>

							</div>

								
